  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Dragonado rarely gets stuck on a problem, unless of course, if it's a XOR Problem.\n\nDragonado while coming back from the Fruit Market, finds an array AAA of NNN elements. He wants to find 444 integers, say i1,i2,i3i_1,i_2,i_3i1​,i2​,i3​ and i4i_4i4​, such that:\n\n\npopcount(Ai1⊕Ai2)=popcount(Ai3⊕Ai4)\\texttt{popcount}(A_{i_1} \\oplus A_{i_2}) = \\texttt{popcount}(A_{i_3} \\oplus A_{i_4})popcount(Ai1​​⊕Ai2​​)=popcount(Ai3​​⊕Ai4​​)\n1≤i1,i2,i3,i4≤N1 \\le i_1,i_2,i_3,i_4 \\le N1≤i1​,i2​,i3​,i4​≤N and  i1,i2,i3,i4i_1,i_2,i_3,i_4i1​,i2​,i3​,i4​ are pairwise distinct.\nHere, popcount(x)\\texttt{popcount}(x)popcount(x) denotes the number of set bits in the binary representation of xxx. Secondly, ⊕\\oplus⊕ denotes the bitwise XOR operation.\n\nCan you help Dragonado find four integers i1,i2,i3i_1, i_2, i_3i1​,i2​,i3​ and i4i_4i4​ which satisfy the given conditions?\n\nOutput −1-1−1 if no answer exists.\n\nIf multiple answers exist, output **any**.\n\nNote: The input of this problem is large, so use fast input/output methods.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line contains a single integer TTT - the number of test cases. Then the test cases follow.\nThe first line of each test case contains an integer NNN - the size of the array AAA.\nThe second line of each test case contains NNN space-separated integers A1,A2,…,ANA_1, A_2, \\dots, A_NA1​,A2​,…,AN​ denoting the array AAA.\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output i1,i2,i3,i4i_1,i_2,i_3,i_4i1​,i2​,i3​,i4​ which satisfy the given conditions.\n\n\nOutput −1-1−1 if no numbers satisfying the conditions exist.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1041 \\leq T \\leq 10^41≤T≤104",
      "4≤N≤1054 \\leq N \\leq 10^54≤N≤105",
      "1≤Ai≤1091 \\leq A_i \\leq 10^91≤Ai​≤109",
      "It is guaranteed that sum of NNN over all test cases does not exceed 2⋅1062 \\cdot 10^62⋅106"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "2\n4\n1 2 5 6\n4\n1 2 3 4",
        "output": "1 4 2 3\n-1",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case-1: 1,4,2,31, 4, 2, 31,4,2,3 is a valid answer because:\n\npopcount(A1⊕A4)=popcount(7)=3\\texttt{popcount}(A_{1} \\oplus A_{4}) = \\texttt{popcount}(7) = 3popcount(A1​⊕A4​)=popcount(7)=3\npopcount(A2⊕A3)=popcount(7)=3\\texttt{popcount}(A_{2} \\oplus A_{3}) = \\texttt{popcount}(7) = 3popcount(A2​⊕A3​)=popcount(7)=3\n\nTherefore, popcount(Ai1⊕Ai2)=popcount(Ai3⊕Ai4)\\texttt{popcount}(A_{i_1} \\oplus A_{i_2}) = \\texttt{popcount}(A_{i_3} \\oplus A_{i_4})popcount(Ai1​​⊕Ai2​​)=popcount(Ai3​​⊕Ai4​​)\nTest case-2: We can prove that no i1,i2,i3,i4i_1, i_2, i_3, i_4i1​,i2​,i3​,i4​ exist that satisfy the given conditions."
      }
    ]
